<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand-Pré</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grand-Pré is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> Basin, that was a major Acadian settlement from the 1680s. Its farmers reclaimed tidal marshes with dykes and sluices. In 1755 the British deported its population, an event remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Longfellow</a>'s poem Evangeline. Its landscape of dykelands was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2012.</p></div>
